1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,968
Before naming a lower-origin rhythm, decide whether it is late and
protective or early and ectopic. A junctional escape may prevent a
prolonged pause after sinus failure. A premature ventricular beat
instead interrupts the expected schedule. The origin alone does not
explain the beat’s role.

2
00:00:19,968 --> 00:00:37,608
Use the backup hierarchy as a reference, then assess the actual
patient. An escape rate may be inadequate for one patient but
temporarily tolerated by another. Treating a protective rhythm as
something to suppress can remove the remaining source of ventricular
activation.

3
00:00:37,608 --> 00:00:57,816
An AV-junctional impulse may activate the atria backwards. A
retrograde P may therefore be inverted before QRS, hidden within it or
seen afterwards. The QRS is often narrow because the normal
ventricular conduction system is still used, unless another conduction
abnormality is present.

4
00:00:57,816 --> 00:01:17,688
The school calls forty to sixty an escape rhythm, sixty to one hundred
accelerated junctional rhythm, and above one hundred junctional
tachycardia. Consider sinus suppression, vagal effects, ischemia,
digoxin exposure or postoperative context, and assess whether
perfusion is sufficient.

5
00:01:17,688 --> 00:01:38,352
A PVC is an early ventricular complex, typically wide and unusual in
shape, without an associated conducted P beforehand. A compensatory
pause may follow, but not every PVC has the textbook pause. Describe
what is present rather than forcing every feature into a fixed
template.

6
00:01:38,352 --> 00:02:01,320
Unifocal PVCs share a shape; multifocal ectopy has differing shapes.
Bigeminy alternates an ectopic beat with another beat, and couplets
are two consecutive PVCs. A beat falling during vulnerable
repolarization raises concern. Check the patient and reversible causes
instead of treating the pattern in isolation.

7
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:21,816
Monomorphic VT shows a rapid sequence of broadly similar ventricular
complexes. The school gives a typical range of one hundred fifty to
two hundred fifty, but slower ventricular tachycardias exist. A
dangerous wide-complex rhythm cannot be dismissed because it falls
outside a memorized range.

8
00:02:21,816 --> 00:02:43,920
Assess responsiveness, breathing and pulse promptly. Pulseless VT
follows the cardiac-arrest pathway. A pulse-present wide-complex
tachycardia requires urgent stability assessment and authorized
expertise; recognizing it does not authorize a PCP to select
cardioversion or antiarrhythmic medication independently.

9
00:02:43,920 --> 00:03:03,048
Ventricular fibrillation is disorganized ventricular activity without
useful coordinated beats. In true sustained VF, the patient has no
effective pulse. If someone is alert with a reproducible pulse while a
lead looks chaotic, investigate artifact without abandoning
assessment.

10
00:03:03,048 --> 00:03:21,912
For an unresponsive pulseless patient, promptly follow the applicable
resuscitation and defibrillation protocol. Technical confirmation and
checks of the leads must not become a prolonged interruption of CPR.
The patient’s condition and the shockable pattern guide action
together.

11
00:03:21,912 --> 00:03:41,712
An idioventricular escape rhythm is a slow ventricular backup,
generally without preceding conducted P waves and with a wide QRS. The
school uses twenty to forty per minute as a reference. It may indicate
profound failure of higher pacing or conduction and inadequate cardiac
output.

12
00:03:41,712 --> 00:04:01,248
Accelerated idioventricular rhythm is the same ventricular-origin
pattern at a faster rate, about forty to one hundred in this teaching.
It can occur around reperfusion and may be tolerated. Do not suppress
it merely to obtain a sinus-looking monitor; assess the underlying
cause and circulation.

13
00:04:01,248 --> 00:04:20,880
Before accepting a flat line as asystole, verify electrode and cable
contact, suitable gain and another lead when feasible. A disconnected
cable or fine VF can mislead. Do these checks within the resuscitation
workflow, not instead of treating a patient without circulation.

14
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:40,608
Organized electrical activity without a pulse is a different branch,
pulseless electrical activity. It is not proof of circulation and is
not made shockable simply by having visible complexes. Follow the
applicable arrest pathway and assess causes without prolonged
diagnostic delays.

15
00:04:40,608 --> 00:04:59,184
The school cause groups include oxygenation problems, electrolyte
disturbance, ischemia, structural disease and medication effects. Use
them to direct questions and assessment, not to diagnose a potassium
value from an isolated wave. Treatable causes may coexist.

16
00:04:59,184 --> 00:05:15,888
Communicate a useful sentence: a suspected ventricular-origin pattern
at an estimated rate, with or without a pulse, plus the patient’s
signs of perfusion and deterioration. That report is more actionable
than a rhythm name detached from the patient.
